<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Munich Secession</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1892">1892</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> Secession was an association of artists founded in 1892 by painters, among them Franz von Stuck, who broke away from the official Munich Artists' Association in protest at its conservative exhibitions. The first of the secessions in the German-speaking world, it inspired those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> and helped make Munich a leading centre of modern art around 1900.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/munich-secession.jpg" alt="Munich Secession" width="320" height="218" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Franz von Stuck (1863-1928) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Internationale_Kunstausstellung_Muenchen_1897.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>By the end of the 19th century more artists lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> than in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> together, but its art world was dominated by the conservative Munich Artists' Association and its allies in government, whose outlook was set out by the so-called Prince of Painters, Franz von Lenbach. In 1891 Prince-Regent Luitpold of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> founded an art foundation devoted to traditional history painting in the service of the state and opposed to impressionism, symbolism and other new trends. The complete financial failure of the association's exhibition of 1888 at the Glaspalast had already led to a bitter dispute that drew in the Ministry of State for Science and Art.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>On 4 April 1892 ninety-six artists who had resigned from the official association founded the Association of Visual Artists of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>, with Bruno Piglhein as president and Paul Hoecker as secretary; within months the name Munich Secession took over. Its first backers were the journalist Georg Hirth, who coined the word secession for the spirit of the new art movements, the socialist editor Georg von Vollmar and the liberal Hans Veit zu Toerring-Jettenbach. The founders called for artists' freedom to present their work directly to the public and acted as a cooperative to secure commissions and their livelihood.</p><p>After a first exhibition at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>'s National Exhibition Building early in 1893, the group opened its own building on land given by Franz von Brandl at the corner of Prinzregentenstraße and Pilotystraße. Its debut there on 16 July 1893 showed 876 works by 297 artists. In 1897 it received the exhibition building on the Königsplatz. Dissatisfied members left to form <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">Phalanx</a> in 1901, and another split in 1913 produced the New Munich Secession. After the National Socialist party brought the arts under their control from 1933, the Secession was dissolved in 1938 as part of the so-called cultural cleansing.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Painters such as Lovis Corinth, Lesser Ury and Gabriel von Max exhibited with the Secession and counted themselves part of it without being founding members. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> new groups founded in 1946 led to the Federal Association of Visual Artists. The Secession marked its centenary in 1992, and in March of the following year a society of friends and sponsors was created to keep its gallery and exhibitions going.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Munich Secession” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 1900 Munich was a leading art city: the <b>Munich Secession</b> was founded in 1892, the magazine Jugend gave its name to German Art Nouveau, and the Blue Rider group of Wassily Kandinsky and Franz Marc formed there in 1911.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bavaria">Kingdom of Bavaria · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
